## CURRENT CODEBASE STATE

Your setup uses TWO parallel transcoding pipelines:
1. **video-transcode.mjs** (legacy): Single H.264 MP4 + all audio tracks multiplexed + 3 MB chunks
2. **video-mse.mjs** (newer MSE): Separate video (H.264 fMP4) + per-language audio (AAC fMP4) + fragment index for seek/switch

StreamVideoMSE.jsx reads manifest type 'mse-av' with:
- video: {manifest, codecs:'avc1.640028', fragIndex}  
- audio[]: {manifest, codecs:'mp4a.40.2', lang, label, fragIndex}
- subs: [{path, lang, label}] as external WebVTT
- probe: {duration}

**Critical MSE implementation**: /api/stream serves chunked manifests via HTTP Range (206 partial content), using 3 MB WINDOW size. Seeks reconstruct via byteForTime (fragment index preferred, linear approximation fallback).

## FORMAT COMPATIBILITY MATRIX (2026)

| Codec | Container | Chrome | Firefox | Safari | Notes |
|-------|-----------|--------|---------|--------|-------|
| **H.264** | MP4 | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ Hardware accel | Most compatible, 25-year maturity, software fallback everywhere |
| **VP9** | WebM | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ M1+ only | ~44% bitrate savings vs H.264 at 4K; no HLS native support (DASH only) |
| **AV1** | WebM/MP4 | ✓ (partial) | ✓ (partial) | ✓ M3+ hardware only | 91.5% decoder support desktop, but macOS Safari ~24%, iOS ~33% |
| **H.265** | MP4 | ✗ web | Limited | ✓ Modern Apple | Patent issues; not recommended for web |

**Recommendation**: Dual-encode H.264 (fMP4 fragmented, universal fallback) + VP9 (DASH, bandwidth conscious users). AV1 too immature for 2026 until iPhone 14S equivalent matures (Q4 2025+).

## NATIVE BROWSER PLAYBACK CAPABILITIES

**Multi-Audio Track Support**:
- HTML5 `<video>` audioTracks API: **NOT reliably implemented** (Safari only, partially). Chrome/Firefox don't populate it.
- **Solution**: You must use Media Source Extensions (MSE) SourceBuffer switching, exactly as StreamVideoMSE does.

**Subtitle Support**:
- HTML5 `<track>` elements: ✓ All browsers
- WebVTT (.vtt) format: ✓ Standard, supported everywhere
- Embedded MP4 subtitles (tx3g): Chrome/Firefox ignore; Safari honors selectively
- **Solution**: External sidecars (.vtt) in separate GitHub repo/shard (current approach is correct)

**Seek & Adaptive Quality**:
- Media Source Extensions: ✓ All modern browsers since 2018
- Fragment-level precision: Requires **fragIndex** (moof baseMediaDecodeTime → wall-clock time mapping)
- Byte-linear approximation: Unreliable in variable-bitrate streams → use fragIndex always

## FFMPEG TRANSCODING COMMANDS

### Strategy A: Single Universal H.264/MP4 (fMP4 fragmented + faststart)
**Best for**: Simplicity, maximum compatibility, embedded audio/subs planning

```bash
# Probe source
ffprobe -v error -show_format -show_streams -of json "$INPUT" > probe.json

# Video + all audio + faststart (keyframe fragmentation)
ffmpeg -y -i "$INPUT" \
  -map 0:v:0 -c:v libx264 -preset fast -crf 21 -pix_fmt yuv420p \
  -map 0:a -c:a aac -b:a 160k \
  -movflags +faststart+frag_keyframe+empty_moov+default_base_moof \
  output.mp4

# Extract WebVTT subtitles (separate files)
for i in $(ffprobe -v error -select_streams s -show_entries stream=index -of csv=p=0 "$INPUT"); do
  ffmpeg -y -i "$INPUT" -map "0:s:$i" -c:s webvtt "sub${i}.vtt"
done

# Chunk into 3 MB pieces + fragment index
node scripts/chunk-and-index.mjs output.mp4 --size 3145728 --out media/chunks
```

**Pros**: Single file, browsers play natively, hardware accelerated everywhere, lowest file size  
**Cons**: Audio multiplexing requires client-side switching via MSE, can't change bitrate per track

### Strategy B: Separated Streams (Multi-Audio + Subtitles, MSE-ready)
**Best for**: Language switching, fine-grain quality control, your current StreamVideoMSE.jsx

```bash
