**Pros**: Independent track selection (no re-download), precise seeking via fragIndex, bitrate/codec per track possible  
**Cons**: Multiple requests, more complex manifest structure, MSE code required

### Strategy C: VP9 WebM + Opus (Bandwidth-Conscious, DASH Streaming)
**Best for**: Modern browsers, 4K content, CDN cost reduction (44% bitrate vs H.264)

```bash
# VP9 encoding (slow, high quality, variable bitrate)
ffmpeg -y -i "$INPUT" \
  -c:v libvpx-vp9 -crf 23 -b:v 0 \
  -row-mt 1 -speed 2 \
  -tile-columns 2 -tile-rows 1 \
  output.webm

# Opus audio (superior low-bitrate audio quality)
ffmpeg -y -i "$INPUT" \
  -map 0:a -vn \
  -c:a libopus -b:a 96k \
  audio.opus

# WebM container (audio embedded inside)
ffmpeg -y -i output.webm -i audio.opus \
  -c:v copy -c:a copy -shortest \
  output_with_audio.webm
```

**Bitrate Reference** (VP9, CRF 23):
- 1080p: 2500 kbps (vs H.264 3500 kbps)
- 2160p/4K: 6000 kbps (vs H.264 15000+ kbps)

**Opus Reference** (psychoacoustic transparency):
- Stereo: 96 kbps (vs AAC 160 kbps)
- Surround 5.1: 192 kbps (vs AAC 320 kbps)

**Browser Support**: Chrome, Firefox, Safari 14.1+ (2021+)  
**Limitation**: DASH-only for adaptive bitrate; HLS mandate is H.264/H.265

